<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Jefferson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1743–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Jefferson was a Virginian planter, lawyer and statesman, the principal author of the Declaration of Independence in 1776 and the third President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1801 to 1809. As president he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803, doubling the size of the country. He founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and championed religious freedom, while owning more than 600 enslaved people during his life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson.jpg" alt="Thomas Jefferson" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rembrandt Peale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Presidential_portrait_of_Thomas_Jefferson_(by_Rembrandt_Peale,_1800).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 July 1776 the Second Continental Congress adopted the Declaration of Independence, drafted mainly by <b>Thomas Jefferson</b>, which justified separation by appeal to natural rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In America, <b>Thomas Jefferson</b> drafted the United States Declaration of Independence of 1776, and James Madison took a leading part in framing the constitution of 1787.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Even <b>Thomas Jefferson</b> agreed, in a letter to John Adams in 1813, that there was a “natural aristocracy” among men, grounded in “virtue and talents”, although he wanted it chosen by election rather than by birth.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the original Constitution the runner-up in the electoral college became vice-president, so in 1796 John Adams found his chief rival, <b>Thomas Jefferson</b>, serving as his deputy; the Twelfth Amendment of 1804 ended the arrangement.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) held that the civil magistrate’s power does not extend to the salvation of souls, and <b>Thomas Jefferson</b>’s “wall of separation between Church and State” (1802) became the American shorthand for the opposite principle.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The American founders often equated direct democracy with mob rule, <b>Thomas Jefferson</b> warned that “an elective despotism is not the government we fought for”, and Richard Ellis and Michael Nelson argue that much constitutional thought from Madison to Lincoln turned on “the problem of majority tyranny”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
